import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse('{"title":"Tagged Values","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Tagged Values","type":"adr","date":"2026-09-01T00:00:00.000Z","status":"accepted","status_note":"Accepted\\n\\nRefines [ADR-0054 — Generic Typecheck Engine](./0054-generic-typecheck-engine.md)\\n(consumes tagged values for type representation) and [ADR-0052 — Arrow\\nCalculus Kernel](./0052-arrow-calculus-kernel.md) (`Value` is\\n`serde_json::Value`). A general host primitive, external to the typechecker.\\n\\nProposed — the type tag set and payload field layout are pinned in the\\nADR-0054 value-encoding spike. This ADR establishes the primitive (tag\\nregistries + `tag`/`match_tag`/`tag_registry`); ADR-0054 consumes it for\\ntypes via the host type registry."},"headers":[],"relativePath":"handl/project/adrs/0055-branded-values.md","filePath":"vendored/handl/project/adrs/0055-branded-values.md"}'),a={name:`handl/project/adrs/0055-branded-values.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="adr-0055-tagged-values" tabindex="-1">ADR-0055: Tagged Values <a class="header-anchor" href="#adr-0055-tagged-values" aria-label="Permalink to “ADR-0055: Tagged Values”">​</a></h1><h2 id="context" tabindex="-1">Context <a class="header-anchor" href="#context" aria-label="Permalink to “Context”">​</a></h2><p>ADR-0032 establishes that types are values: the typechecker and runtime share one value space. This creates a question: how is the type <code>number</code> distinguished from the string <code>&quot;number&quot;</code>, if both are values?</p><p>The same need arises beyond types — any value-level concept that must stay distinct from an ordinary value of the same shape:</p><ul><li>A <strong>type</strong> vs the value that denotes its name.</li><li>A <strong>unit of measure</strong> (<code>Meters(5)</code> vs <code>Seconds(5)</code> — same number, different meaning).</li><li>A <strong>capability handle</strong> (<code>fs</code> vs the string <code>&quot;fs&quot;</code>).</li><li><strong>Provenance/ownership</strong> (a value marked with where it came from).</li></ul><p>This is the same problem JavaScript&#39;s <code>Symbol</code> solves for keys: <code>Symbol(&quot;number&quot;)</code> and the string <code>&quot;number&quot;</code> never collide. handl needs the analogous property for values.</p><h3 id="why-not-a-magic-key-convention-rejected" tabindex="-1">Why not a magic-key convention (rejected) <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-not-a-magic-key-convention-rejected" aria-label="Permalink to “Why not a magic-key convention (rejected)”">​</a></h3><p>A prior draft proposed branded values as a convention on JSON: a reserved <code>__brand__</code> key (<code>{&quot;__brand__&quot;:&quot;type.collection&quot;,&quot;name&quot;:&quot;number&quot;}</code>). This is rejected for one reason:</p><p><strong>Forgery.</strong> A convention on JSON is forgeable. Any code can write <code>{&quot;__brand__&quot;:&quot;type.collection&quot;,...}</code>. In a capability-secure language (ADR-0046), a forgeable tagged value is a hole: user code could construct a value that claims to be a type the typechecker did not produce. The fix is to make tagging a <strong>host capability</strong>, not a user-constructible convention. A value is tagged iff the host minted it; user code cannot forge a tag.</p><h3 id="why-not-bare-string-tags-rejected" tabindex="-1">Why not bare string tags (rejected) <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-not-bare-string-tags-rejected" aria-label="Permalink to “Why not bare string tags (rejected)”">​</a></h3><p>A second draft used bare string tags: <code>tag(&quot;type.pi&quot;, payload)</code>. This is rejected for one reason:</p><p><strong>Namespace collisions.</strong> String tags can collide across kits. Two kits that both define <code>&quot;type.pi&quot;</code> (or <code>&quot;unit.meters&quot;</code>, or <code>&quot;capability.fs&quot;</code>) produce indistinguishable tags even when they mean different things. There is no way to keep two <code>type.pi</code> from different sources distinct, or to assert that two <code>type.pi</code> from the same source are the same, beyond string equality.</p><h3 id="what-dependent-type-languages-do" tabindex="-1">What dependent-type languages do <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-dependent-type-languages-do" aria-label="Permalink to “What dependent-type languages do”">​</a></h3><p>Agda, Idris, and Lean treat types as first-class values you can pass, compute with, and return — but the <em>representation</em> of a type value is the typechecker&#39;s, owned internally. User code cannot forge a type value; it can only produce one by evaluating a type expression through the typechecker&#39;s normalizer. handl adopts the same property, generalized: tagging is a host capability that mints unforgeable tagged values for any purpose, and tags carry <strong>registry identity</strong> so collisions across sources are impossible.</p><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to “Decision”">​</a></h2><p>Introduce <strong>tagged values</strong> as a general host primitive, backed by <strong>tag registries</strong>. A tagged value is a value carrying a structural tag minted by a registry. <code>tag</code> is a <strong>host capability</strong> (offered by the host, not user-constructible) — only the host mints tags, so tagged values are unforgeable. Tags carry <strong>registry identity</strong>, so two tags from different registries never collide even if their names match. Equality depends on the registry&#39;s semantics (structural or nominal — see §4).</p><h3 id="_1-tag-registries" tabindex="-1">1. Tag registries <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-tag-registries" aria-label="Permalink to “1. Tag registries”">​</a></h3><p>A <strong>tag registry</strong> is an identity that owns a namespace of tags. There are two kinds:</p><ul><li><strong>Host registry</strong>: created by the host at startup. The typechecker&#39;s <code>type.*</code> tags live in a host registry. The host owns its identity; user code cannot create or impersonate it.</li><li><strong>Code-level registry</strong>: created by user code via a host capability (<code>tag_registry()</code>). Each call to <code>tag_registry()</code> mints a fresh, unique registry identity. Used for user-defined nominal types, units, capabilities, provenance — anything that needs distinctness by identity.</li></ul><p>A registry is itself a value (a tagged value minted by the host, e.g. <code>{&quot;__tag__&quot;:&quot;registry&quot;,&quot;id&quot;:7}</code>) that can be passed around, stored, and closed over. Its identity is its minted <code>id</code>; two registries are equal iff they are the same minted registry.</p><h3 id="_2-tag-match-tag-tag-registry-—-host-capabilities" tabindex="-1">2. <code>tag</code> / <code>match_tag</code> / <code>tag_registry</code> — host capabilities <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-tag-match-tag-tag-registry-—-host-capabilities" aria-label="Permalink to “2. tag / match_tag / tag_registry — host capabilities”">​</a></h3><p>The value language gains three <code>Arr</code> ops (ADR-0052), offered by the host as capabilities:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Op</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>tag_registry()</code></td><td>mint a fresh code-level registry. Host capability — not callable by arbitrary user code unless granted.</td></tr><tr><td><code>tag(registry, name, payload)</code></td><td>mint a tagged value: <code>{&quot;__tag__&quot;: name, &quot;__registry__&quot;: registry, …payload}</code>. Host capability — only the host mints tags.</td></tr><tr><td><code>match_tag(v, cases)</code></td><td>dispatch on the tag of <code>v</code>; cases bind the payload fields. Available to any code that holds <code>v</code>.</td></tr></tbody></table><p><code>tag</code> and <code>tag_registry</code> are minting capabilities (host-secured); <code>match_tag</code> is the inspection op (anyone holding a tagged value can read its tag and fields). This matches capability discipline: the power to <em>create</em> a tagged value or registry is restricted; the power to <em>inspect</em> a held value is not.</p><h3 id="_3-representation-—-tagged-values-are-serde-json-value" tabindex="-1">3. Representation — tagged values are <code>serde_json::Value</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-representation-—-tagged-values-are-serde-json-value" aria-label="Permalink to “3. Representation — tagged values are serde_json::Value”">​</a></h3><p>A tagged value is a JSON object with two reserved keys:</p><div class="language-"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>{&quot;__tag__&quot;: &quot;type.collection&quot;, &quot;__registry__&quot;: {&quot;id&quot;: 0}, &quot;name&quot;: &quot;number&quot;}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>{&quot;__tag__&quot;: &quot;type.pi&quot;, &quot;__registry__&quot;: {&quot;id&quot;: 0}, &quot;param&quot;: …, &quot;body&quot;: …}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>{&quot;__tag__&quot;: &quot;meters&quot;, &quot;__registry__&quot;: {&quot;id&quot;: 7}, &quot;value&quot;: 5}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>{&quot;__tag__&quot;: &quot;fs&quot;, &quot;__registry__&quot;: {&quot;id&quot;: 12}, &quot;name&quot;: &quot;fs&quot;}</span></span></code></pre></div><p><code>__tag__</code> is the tag name; <code>__registry__</code> is the registry identity that minted it; the remaining fields are the payload. The value is still a <code>serde_json::Value</code>, so ADR-0052&#39;s &quot;no new value type is invented&quot; holds: tagging is a convention on the existing value space, secured by host capability and disambiguated by registry identity.</p><h3 id="_4-two-equality-flavors-one-primitive" tabindex="-1">4. Two equality flavors, one primitive <a class="header-anchor" href="#_4-two-equality-flavors-one-primitive" aria-label="Permalink to “4. Two equality flavors, one primitive”">​</a></h3><p>The registry determines equality semantics:</p><ul><li><strong>Structural equality</strong> (host type registry): two tagged values are equal iff their <code>__tag__</code>, <code>__registry__</code>, and payload fields are equal. The typechecker&#39;s host registry is a single fixed identity, so two <code>type.collection</code>/<code>number</code> tags from it unify by structure — exactly what the typechecker needs for base types, Pi/Sigma/Forall.</li><li><strong>Nominal-by-registry identity</strong> (code-level registries): two tagged values are equal iff their <code>__registry__</code> identities are the same minted registry <em>and</em> their <code>__tag__</code> names match. Two <code>meters</code> tags from <em>different</em> code-level registries are <strong>not equal</strong> even if names and payloads match — they are distinct nominal types. Two <code>meters</code> tags from the <em>same</em> code-level registry are equal. This is what makes user-defined types, units, and capabilities distinct by identity.</li></ul><p>The typechecker uses the host registry (structural). User code uses code-level registries (nominal). Both use the same <code>tag</code>/<code>match_tag</code> primitive; the registry parameter selects the equality flavor.</p><h3 id="_5-forgery-is-impossible" tabindex="-1">5. Forgery is impossible <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-forgery-is-impossible" aria-label="Permalink to “5. Forgery is impossible”">​</a></h3><p>User code cannot construct <code>{&quot;__tag__&quot;:&quot;type.collection&quot;,...}</code> because <code>tag</code> is a host capability. The host mints tagged values; user code receives them (by evaluating a type expression, calling a unit constructor, etc.) and can pass them around, but cannot forge one. A user object that happens to have <code>__tag__</code> and <code>__registry__</code> keys is not a tagged value — only values minted by the host <code>tag</code> op are. The runtime/typechecker distinguish minted tagged values from hand-constructed objects by provenance (the host tracks which values it minted), not by the keys alone.</p><h3 id="_6-sugar-—-type-and-future-unit-etc" tabindex="-1">6. Sugar — <code>type</code> (and future <code>unit</code>, etc.) <a class="header-anchor" href="#_6-sugar-—-type-and-future-unit-etc" aria-label="Permalink to “6. Sugar — type (and future unit, etc.)”">​</a></h3><p>Surface sugar applies a tag by name. <code>type</code> tags a value as a type:</p><div class="language-"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>type MyNumber</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  ⟶  MyNumber = tag(type_registry(), &quot;type.nominal&quot;, { name: &quot;MyNumber&quot; })</span></span>
<span class="line"><span></span></span>
<span class="line"><span>type MyNumber = number</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  ⟶  MyNumber = number   // alias: MyNumber is the structural type \`number\`</span></span></code></pre></div><p><code>type X</code> (no <code>=</code>) creates a <strong>nominal</strong> type tagged with its name in the host type registry — distinct from every other type by name. <code>type X = T</code> creates a <strong>structural alias</strong> — <code>X</code> is just <code>T</code>. The sugar desugars to <code>tag</code> calls against the host type registry; it introduces no new value representation. (Future sugar: <code>unit meters</code>, <code>capability fs</code>, etc., desugar to <code>tag</code> calls against code-level registries minted for that purpose.)</p><h3 id="_7-untagged-values-are-unchanged" tabindex="-1">7. Untagged values are unchanged <a class="header-anchor" href="#_7-untagged-values-are-unchanged" aria-label="Permalink to “7. Untagged values are unchanged”">​</a></h3><p>Plain <code>serde_json::Value</code>s (strings, numbers, bools, arrays, untagged objects) are exactly as today. Tagging is opt-in: a value is tagged iff the host minted it. The runtime and existing eval rules see no difference unless they call <code>match_tag</code>.</p><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to “Consequences”">​</a></h2><h3 id="positive" tabindex="-1">Positive <a class="header-anchor" href="#positive" aria-label="Permalink to “Positive”">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>No forgery.</strong> Tagged values are unforgeable: <code>tag</code> is a host capability, not user-constructible. The capability boundary (ADR-0046) is preserved.</li><li><strong>No namespace collisions.</strong> Tags carry registry identity; two tags from different registries never collide even if their names match. Kits can define tags without coordinating string namespaces.</li><li><strong>Two equality flavors, one primitive.</strong> Structural equality (host type registry) for the typechecker&#39;s base types and Pi/Sigma/Forall; nominal equality (code-level registries) for user-defined types, units, capabilities. Same <code>tag</code>/<code>match_tag</code> primitive; the registry selects the flavor.</li><li><strong>A general primitive.</strong> Tagging serves types, units, capabilities, and provenance with one mechanism. Adding a new tagged concept is a new registry or tag namespace, not a new value type or engine change.</li><li><strong>Types are values, without collision.</strong> <code>number</code> (the type) is a tagged value in the host type registry; <code>&quot;number&quot;</code> (the string) is the string. The typechecker distinguishes them while keeping both in the shared value space — ADR-0032 honored literally, ADR-0054 unblocked.</li><li><strong>No new value type.</strong> Tagging is a convention on <code>serde_json::Value</code>: ADR-0052&#39;s &quot;no new value type is invented&quot; holds. The eval engine produces and consumes tagged values as ordinary JSON objects.</li><li><strong>Sugar for the common case.</strong> <code>type X</code> / <code>type X = T</code> desugar to <code>tag</code> against the host type registry, keeping the surface language readable.</li></ul><h3 id="negative" tabindex="-1">Negative <a class="header-anchor" href="#negative" aria-label="Permalink to “Negative”">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>Reserved keys.</strong> <code>__tag__</code> and <code>__registry__</code> are reserved at the value level. User objects that happen to use them are not tagged values (provenance distinguishes them), but the keys are still reserved. Mitigation: the keys are double-underscore-prefixed (matching the existing <code>__ast__</code> convention from ADR-0047); the parser/eval can reject user <code>__tag__</code>/<code>__registry__</code> keys or namespace them.</li><li><strong>Provenance tracking.</strong> The host must track which values it minted, so a hand-constructed <code>{&quot;__tag__&quot;:...}</code> is not mistaken for a minted tagged value. This is a small bookkeeping cost in the host.</li><li><strong>Registry bookkeeping.</strong> The host must mint and track registry identities (host + code-level). This is a small cost; registries are lightweight (just an <code>id</code>).</li><li><strong>Two equality flavors.</strong> Structural and nominal equality coexist; rule authors and the typechecker must know which a registry uses. Mitigation: the host type registry is always structural; code-level registries are always nominal. The flavor is determined by the registry, not chosen per tag.</li><li><strong>Tagged values are heavier.</strong> A tagged value is an object with two reserved keys, not a bare primitive. Type values that were a single <code>Name</code> in the old nbe are now a small object. This is the cost of unification; acceptable given the typechecker runs at compile time on a small number of type values.</li><li><strong><code>match_tag</code> is a new dispatch op.</strong> Engines (eval, typecheck) must implement it. It is a small addition alongside the existing <code>Arr</code> ops.</li></ul><h3 id="scope-boundary-—-what-this-adr-does-not-promise" tabindex="-1">Scope boundary — what this ADR does NOT promise <a class="header-anchor" href="#scope-boundary-—-what-this-adr-does-not-promise" aria-label="Permalink to “Scope boundary — what this ADR does NOT promise”">​</a></h3><ul><li>It does <strong>not</strong> introduce nominal-by-reference identity for <em>objects</em>. Nominal equality is by <em>registry</em> identity, not by value reference; two tagged values from the same registry with the same name and payload are equal.</li><li>It does <strong>not</strong> define the full set of type tags (<code>type.collection</code>, <code>type.pi</code>, …). That is the ADR-0054 value-encoding spike.</li><li>It does <strong>not</strong> define unit/capability/provenance sugar beyond noting they reuse the same primitive with code-level registries. Those are future work.</li><li>It does <strong>not</strong> change the runtime value representation — tagging is a convention on <code>serde_json::Value</code>, secured by host capability and disambiguated by registry identity.</li></ul>`,47)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};